#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119808 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "aaefbc53-9df9-54a0-a969-66e062553d5c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119809 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a04b551f-3323-5b91-b01e-941912cc92be" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Vector Lies on a Plane"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119856 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fac58a79-2d3f-5ad3-b1ab-c5ce279c4ebe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Value of an Unknown Such That a Vector Lies in the Span of Given Vectors"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121035 :tutorial/content "Given two vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ the set of all possible linear combinations of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ x_1 \\mathbf{a}_1 + x_2 \\mathbf{a}_2 \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nThe set $U$ is also called the **linear span** of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ and is denoted by $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$ We say that $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ are the **generators** of $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the linear span generated by the following vectors:\n$$\n\\mathbf{a_1} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}, \\quad\n\t\\mathbf{a_2} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ - 1\\end{array} \\right]} .\n$$\n\nFrom the definition of linear span, we know that if $\\mathbf{b} \\in \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ then $\\mathbf{b}$ must take the form\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{b} = x_1\\mathbf{a_1} + x_2\\mathbf{a_2}\n$$\n\n for some $x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={x}_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+{x}_{2}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{1} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3{x}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1}+2{x}_{2} \\\\ -{x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the linear span of $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2$ is\n$$\nU = \\left\\{ {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- {x}_{1} + 3 {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{1} + 2 {x}_{2} \\\\ - {x}_{2}\\end{array} \\right]} \\: : \\: x_1,x_2\\in\\mathbb{R} \\right\\}.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ can be viewed as the plane $\\Pi$ that passes through the origin and is parallel to the vectors $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5b202475e0bc3ebb07df1f07bab72e9e74b13a99196c2a8a5adb624a7534a03.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c4e2e270-f1fb-5633-9239-4696e8f0a25b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3193 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121036 :tutorial/content "The linear span of vectors can be interpreted geometrically as the points in space that can be reached by adding multiples of those vectors.\n\n- The span of a single nonzero vector is a *line* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\neq \\mathbf{0},$ then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ determines the *line* with direction vector $\\mathbf{a}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a} \\}$ represents the line $l$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/08/080aa6203494e915e28c491a3d86176f7ba634bcbdfe40b094c07d85fb29bb69.png)\n\n- The span of two non-parallel vectors is a *plane* through the origin. More precisely, if $\\mathbf{a} \\not\\parallel \\mathbf{b}$ ($\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel), then $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$ determines the *plane* with direction vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}$ that passes through the origin. For instance, if\n$$\n\\mathbf{a} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 \\\\ - 1 \\\\ 2\\end{array} \\right]},\n\t\\qquad\n\t\\mathbf{b} = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}- 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]},\n$$\nthen $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}, \\mathbf{b} \\}$  represents the plane $\\Pi$ *spanned* by $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{b}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n![](images/56/5606535bd91eb35745a9852ceb451720339105f5a97fb66ccfcb1164ecd55ffd.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c50c93be-7bf2-5511-b456-c60478a53270" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3194 :tutorial/title "Geometric Interpretation of the Linear Span"}]}
